ESP32-V3 host with a Silicon Labs EFR32MG21 (ETH-MG21) or EFR32MG24 (ETH-MG24) Zigbee radio, IP101 Ethernet PHY, Wi-Fi and USB-C. The PoE variants [ETH-MG21 POE / ETH-MG24 POE](https://www.inswift.tech/en/products/eth-mg-poe/) use the same pinout.

The device ships with Inswift's own firmware. Tasmota is an optional alternative for the ESP32: it bridges the EFR32 UART to TCP so ZHA or Zigbee2MQTT can use the gateway over the network. Zigbee2Tasmota has not been tested on this device.

## Flashing

Flash **Tasmota32 Sonoff-ZigbeeBridgePro** (`tasmota32-zbbrdgpro`) over USB-C with the [Tasmota web installer](https://tasmota.github.io/install/) or esptool. This build includes Ethernet and the TCP serial bridge.

## Configuration

Apply the template, then run:

```console
Backlog TCPBaudRate 115200; TCPConfig 8N1; Rule1 ON System#Boot DO TCPStart 6638 ENDON; Rule1 1; Restart 1
```

If Ethernet does not come up after applying the template:

```console
Backlog EthType 1; EthAddress 1; EthClockMode 0; Ethernet 1
```

Port 6638 matches the stock Inswift firmware. The EFR32 runs EmberZNet NCP firmware at 115200 baud, no flow control.

**GPIO33 (BSL) must be Output Lo.** Do not set it to Zigbee Rst 2: the bootloader polarity is the opposite of the Sonoff ZBBridge Pro, and Zigbee Rst 2 can leave the EFR32 stuck in its bootloader.

| GPIO | Function |
|------|----------|
| 0 | Ethernet clock in |
| 2 / 4 / 14 | RGB LED blue / red / green (PWM) |
| 5 | ETH POWER |
| 13 / 17 | TCP Rx / TCP Tx (EFR32 UART) |
| 15 | Zigbee Rst 1 (EFR32 nRESET) |
| 18 / 23 | ETH MDIO / ETH MDC |
| 33 | Output Lo (EFR32 BSL) |
| 34 | Button_n 1 |

## Home Assistant

- Zigbee2MQTT: `port: tcp://GATEWAY_IP:6638`, `adapter: ember`, `baudrate: 115200`
- ZHA: radio type EZSP, serial device path `socket://GATEWAY_IP:6638`

